      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$",
