    "q-69728": {
      "dom_id": "step-q69728",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
