    "q-69736": {
      "dom_id": "step-q69736",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+{t}^{-2}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =2\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j+{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t}))\\,j-({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2})\\,k, \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }2)\\,i-({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the limits one-by-one.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t})=1+{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=1 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2}={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}=0 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }2=2 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t}=0 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =-1\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-i+j, \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =2\\,i+0\\,j+e\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+e\\,k)+2(-i+j) \\\\  & =2j+e\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
