    "q-69730": {
      "dom_id": "step-q69730",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}{t}^{2} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{0} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the third component is a special limit.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}1 \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2(0)-1}{{0}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
