    "q-78268": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78268",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{′}}{(t-1)^{′}}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{1}{t}) \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+0\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
