    "q-78269": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78269",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,2{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{(2t)},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0+2}{0+1},\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the second component of ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)$ we used a special limit ${lim}_{u\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡u}{u}=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{({e}^{2t}-1)^{′}}{(t)^{′}},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{1},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
